    "target_text": "The number of octaves on a harpsichord varies significantly depending on the era, the specific model, and its intended use. Unlike modern pianos, which have a standardized range, harpsichords were built to suit the musical styles and instruments of their time.\n\nHere is a breakdown of the typical ranges:\n\n*   **Historical Baroque Harpsichords (1600–1750):**\n    Most harpsichords from the Baroque period (such as those by Bach, Handel, or Couperin) typically spanned **three to four and a half octaves**.\n    *   A common range was from **C2 to C6** (3 octaves) or **C2 to E6** (3.5 octaves).\n    *   Some larger instruments extended to **C2 to F6** (4 octaves).\n    *   Very rare examples from the late 17th century occasionally reached 5 octaves, but this was not the norm.\n\n*   **Modern and Contemporary Harpsichords:**\n    Modern builders often create instruments with wider ranges to accommodate the full repertoire of the harpsichord, including works by later composers who wrote for the instrument.\n    *   Many modern instruments span **four to five octaves**.\n    *   Some specialized or \"grand\" harpsichords can reach **six octaves** (e.g., from C1 to C7), though these are less common and often require a very large physical space.\n\n**Conclusion:**\n\nWhile there is no single fixed number, a typical harpsichord has **three to four octaves**. However, modern instruments frequently feature **four to five octaves**, and rare large-scale models can extend to **six octaves**."
